                "content": "# Solution\n\n## Part (1): Overall Standard Reduction Potential\n\nThe overall five-electron reduction is:\n\n$$XO_4^- + 8H^+ + 5e^- \\rightarrow X^{2+} + 4H_2O$$\n\n**Why an arithmetic mean fails.** Standard electrode potentials are *intensive* quantities (analogous to chemical potential per mole of electrons). Free energies, by contrast, are *extensive* and additive for sequential steps. The correct procedure is:\n\n$$\\Delta G^\\circ_{\\text{total}} = \\Delta G_1^\\circ + \\Delta G_2^\\circ = -n_1FE_1^\\circ - n_2FE_2^\\circ = -3F(0.900) - 2F(1.200)$$\n\n$$= -F(2.700 + 2.400) = -5.100\\,F$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\\boxed{E^\\circ_{\\text{overall}} = \\frac{n_1E_1^\\circ + n_2E_2^\\circ}{n_1+n_2} = \\frac{3(0.900)+2(1.200)}{5} = \\frac{5.100}{5} = 1.020\\;\\text{V}}$$\n\nAn unweighted arithmetic mean would give $(0.900+1.200)/2 = 1.050$ V, which is **incorrect** because it implicitly assumes each half-reaction contributes one mole of electrons equally. The weighting by $n_i$ is essential.\n\n---\n\n## Part (2): Disproportionation of $XO_2(s)$\n\n**Oxidation half-reaction** (reverse of reaction 1, \u00d72 to balance electrons):\n\n$$2\\,XO_2(s) + 4H_2O \\rightarrow 2\\,XO_4^- + 8H^+ + 6e^-$$\n\n**Reduction half-reaction** (reaction 2, \u00d73):\n\n$$3\\,XO_2(s) + 12H^+ + 6e^- \\rightarrow 3\\,X^{2+} + 6H_2O$$\n\n**Summed and simplified:**\n\n$$\\boxed{5\\,XO_2(s) + 4H^+ \\rightarrow 2\\,XO_4^- + 3\\,X^{2+} + 2\\,H_2O}$$\n\n*Verification:* X: 5 = 2+3 \u2713; O: 10 = 8+2 \u2713; H: 4 = 4 \u2713; Charge: +4 = \u22122+6 \u2713\n\n**Spontaneity under standard conditions:**\n\n$$E_{\\text{cell}}^\\circ = E_{\\text{cathode}}^\\circ - E_{\\text{anode}}^\\circ = E_2^\\circ - E_1^\\circ = 1.200 - 0.900 = +0.300\\;\\text{V} > 0$$\n\nSince $E_{\\text{cell}}^\\circ > 0$, $XO_2(s)$ **is** thermodynamically unstable toward disproportionation under standard conditions.\n\n---\n\n## Part (3): Quantitative Parameters for Disproportionation\n\n| Quantity | Value |\n|---|---|\n| $E_{\\text{cell}}^\\circ$ | $+0.300\\;\\text{V}$ |\n| $n$ | $6$ |\n| $\\log_{10}K$ | $\\dfrac{6\\times 0.300}{0.05916} = \\dfrac{1.800}{0.05916} = 30.43$ |\n| $K$ | $10^{30.43} \\approx 2.7\\times 10^{30}$ |\n\n---\n\n## Part (4): $E_{\\text{cell}}$ as a Function of pH\n\nThe reaction quotient for the balanced disproportionation is:\n\n$$Q = \\frac{a_{XO_4^-}^{\\,2}\\; a_{X^{2+}}^{\\,3}}{a_{H^+}^{\\,4}}$$\n\nWith $a_{XO_4^-}=a_{X^{2+}}=1$ and $a_{H^+}=10^{-\\text{pH}}$:\n\n$$\\log Q = -4\\log a_{H^+} = 4\\,\\text{pH}$$\n\nApplying the Nernst equation:\n\n$$E_{\\text{cell}} = 0.300 - \\frac{0.05916}{6}\\,(4\\,\\text{pH})$$\n\n$$\\boxed{E_{\\text{cell}} = 0.300 - 0.03944\\;\\text{pH}}$$\n\n**Setting $E_{\\text{cell}}=0$:**\n\n$$\\text{pH} = \\frac{0.300}{0.03944} \\approx 7.61$$\n\n**Interpretation:**\n- For $\\text{pH} < 7.61$: $E_{\\text{cell}}>0$ \u2192 disproportionation is **spontaneous** (forward direction).\n- For $\\text{pH} > 7.61$: $E_{\\text{cell}}<0$ \u2192 the reverse (comproportionation) is spontaneous.\n\n---\n\n## Part (5): Specific Conditions at pH = 6.00\n\n$$Q = \\frac{(10^{-3})^2\\;(10^{-2})^3}{(10^{-6})^4} = \\frac{10^{-6}\\cdot 10^{-6}}{10^{-24}} = 10^{12}$$\n\n$$E_{\\text{cell}} = 0.300 - \\frac{0.05916}{6}\\times 12 = 0.300 - 0.1183 = \\boxed{0.182\\;\\text{V}}$$\n\nSince $E_{\\text{cell}}>0$, the **disproportionation proceeds spontaneously** in the forward direction:\n\n$$5\\,XO_2(s) + 4H^+ \\rightarrow 2\\,XO_4^- + 3\\,X^{2+} + 2H_2O$$\n\n---\n\n## Part (6): The Student's Error\n\nThe student's reasoning \u2014 \"$E_2^\\circ > E_1^\\circ$, therefore $XO_2$ disproportionates at every pH and every composition\" \u2014 is flawed because it **confuses a standard-state conclusion with a universal one**.\n\nPrecisely:\n\n1. **$E_2^\\circ > E_1^\\circ$ establishes only that $E_{\\text{cell}}^\\circ > 0$**, i.e., the disproportionation is spontaneous when *all* solute activities equal unity (which forces $a_{H^+}=1$, i.e., pH = 0).\n\n2. **The Nernst equation introduces the reaction quotient $Q$.** For this particular reaction, $Q \\propto a_{H^+}^{-4}$, so increasing pH (decreasing $a_{H^+}$) *increases* $Q$ and *decreases* $E_{\\text{cell}}$. As shown in Part (4), beyond pH \u2248 7.61 the cell potential becomes negative and disproportionation is no longer spontaneous.\n\n3. **Product activities also matter.** High $a_{XO_4^-}$ or $a_{X^{2+}}$ increases $Q$ and can push $E_{\\text{cell}}$ below zero even at pH = 0.\n\nThe standard-potential comparison is a *necessary but not sufficient* criterion for spontaneity under arbitrary conditions; the full Nernst equation must be evaluated.",
                "reasoning_content": "Let me work through this problem step by step.\n\nWe have:\n- $XO_4^-$ with X in +7\n- $XO_2(s)$ with X in +4\n- $X^{2+}$ with X in +2\n\nGiven half-reactions:\n1. $XO_4^- + 4H^+ + 3e^- \\rightarrow XO_2(s) + 2H_2O$, $E_1^\\circ = 0.900$ V\n2. $XO_2(s) + 4H^+ + 2e^- \\rightarrow X^{2+} + 2H_2O$, $E_2^\\circ = 1.200$ V\n\n**(1) Standard reduction potential for the overall five-electron reduction**\n\nThe overall reaction is:\n$XO_4^- + 8H^+ + 5e^- \\rightarrow X^{2+} + 4H_2O$\n\nTo find $E^\\circ$ for this, we use the relationship $\\Delta G^\\circ = -nFE^\\circ$.\n\n$\\Delta G_1^\\circ = -3F(0.900)$\n$\\Delta G_2^\\circ = -2F(1.200)$\n\n$\\Delta G_{\\text{total}}^\\circ = \\Delta G_1^\\circ + \\Delta G_2^\\circ = -3F(0.900) - 2F(1.200) = -F(2.700 + 2.400) = -5F(0.960)$\n\nSo $E_{\\text{overall}}^\\circ = 0.960$ V.\n\nAn unweighted arithmetic mean would give $(0.900 + 1.200)/2 = 1.050$ V, which is wrong. The correct method is to use $\\Delta G^\\circ = -nFE^\\circ$ and combine the free energies (which are additive), not the potentials. The weighted average is:\n\n$E^\\circ_{\\text{overall}} = \\frac{n_1 E_1^\\circ + n_2 E_2^\\circ}{n_1 + n_2} = \\frac{3(0.900) + 2(1.200)}{3+2} = \\frac{2.700 + 2.400}{5} = \\frac{5.100}{5} = 1.020$ V\n\nWait, let me recalculate. $\\Delta G_1^\\circ = -n_1 F E_1^\\circ = -3F(0.900) = -2.700F$\n$\\Delta G_2^\\circ = -n_2 F E_2^\\circ = -2F(1.200) = -2.400F$\n\n$\\Delta G_{\\text{total}}^\\circ = -2.700F - 2.400F = -5.100F$\n\n$E_{\\text{overall}}^\\circ = -\\frac{\\Delta G_{\\text{total}}^\\circ}{n_{\\text{total}} F} = \\frac{5.100F}{5F} = 1.020$ V\n\nSo the correct answer is 1.020 V, not 0.960 V. Let me recheck.\n\n$E_{\\text{overall}}^\\circ = \\frac{n_1 E_1^\\circ + n_2 E_2^\\circ}{n_1 + n_2} = \\frac{3 \\times 0.900 + 2 \\times 1.200}{5} = \\frac{2.7 + 2.4}{5} = \\frac{5.1}{5} = 1.020$ V\n\nYes, 1.020 V.\n\nThe unweighted arithmetic mean is $(0.900 + 1.200)/2 = 1.050$ V, which is wrong. The reason is that standard potentials are intensive quantities (like chemical potentials per mole of electrons), while free energies are extensive. You must combine free energies (which are additive for sequential steps) and then divide by the total number of electrons.\n\n**(2) Disproportionation of $XO_2(s)$**\n\nDisproportionation means $XO_2$ is both oxidized and reduced:\n- Oxidation (reverse of reaction 1): $XO_2(s) + 2H_2O \\rightarrow XO_4^- + 4H^+ + 3e^-$\n- Reduction (reaction 2): $XO_2(s) + 4H^+ + 2e^- \\rightarrow X^{2+} + 2H_2O$\n\nTo balance electrons, multiply oxidation by 2 and reduction by 3:\n- $2XO_2(s) + 4H_2O \\rightarrow 2XO_4^- + 8H^+ + 6e^-$\n- $3XO_2(s) + 12H^+ + 6e^- \\rightarrow 3X^{2+} + 6H_2O$\n\nAdding:\n$5XO_2(s) + 12H^+ + 4H_2O \\rightarrow 2XO_4^- + 8H^+ + 3X^{2+} + 6H_2O$\n\nSimplifying:\n$5XO_2(s) + 4H^+ \\rightarrow 2XO_4^- + 3X^{2+} + 2H_2O$\n\nLet me verify: \n- X: 5 on left, 2+3=5 on right \u2713\n- O: 10 on left, 8+2=10 on right \u2713\n- H: 4 on left, 4 on right \u2713\n- Charge: +4 on left, 2(-1)+3(+2) = -2+6 = +4 on right \u2713\n\nNow, is it thermodynamically unstable toward disproportionation under standard conditions?\n\nFor disproportionation to be spontaneous, we need $E_{\\text{cell}}^\\circ > 0$.\n\nThe cell potential is:\n$E_{\\text{cell}}^\\circ = E_{\\text{cathode}}^\\circ - E_{\\text{anode}}^\\circ$\n\nThe cathode is where reduction occurs: reaction 2, $E_2^\\circ = 1.200$ V\nThe anode is where oxidation occurs: reverse of reaction 1, so the reduction potential is $E_1^\\circ = 0.900$ V\n\n$E_{\\text{cell}}^\\circ = E_2^\\circ - E_1^\\circ = 1.200 - 0.900 = 0.300$ V\n\nSince $E_{\\text{cell}}^\\circ > 0$, yes, $XO_2(s)$ is thermodynamically unstable toward disproportionation under standard conditions.\n\n**(3) For the balanced disproportionation reaction:**\n\n$E_{\\text{cell}}^\\circ = 0.300$ V\n$n = 6$ electrons transferred\n\n$\\log_{10} K = \\frac{nE_{\\text{cell}}^\\circ}{0.05916} = \\frac{6 \\times 0.300}{0.05916} = \\frac{1.800}{0.05916} = 30.426...$\n\nLet me compute this more precisely: $1.800/0.05916 = 30.4260...$\n\nActually, let me be more precise: $1.8/0.05916 = 30.4259...$\n\n$K = 10^{30.426} \\approx 2.67 \\times 10^{30}$\n\nLet me compute: $10^{30.426} = 10^{30} \\times 10^{0.426} = 10^{30} \\times 2.667 \\approx 2.67 \\times 10^{30}$\n\nActually, $10^{0.426}$: $\\log_{10}(2.67) = 0.4265$, so $10^{0.426} \\approx 2.67$.\n\nSo $K \\approx 2.67 \\times 10^{30}$.\n\n**(4) $E_{\\text{cell}}$ as a function of pH when $a_{XO_4^-} = a_{X^{2+}} = 1$**\n\nThe disproportionation reaction is:\n$5XO_2(s) + 4H^+ \\rightarrow 2XO_4^- + 3X^{2+} + 2H_2O$\n\nThe Nernst equation:\n$E_{\\text{cell}} = E_{\\text{cell}}^\\circ - \\frac{0.05916}{n} \\log Q$\n\nwhere $Q = \\frac{a_{XO_4^-}^2 \\cdot a_{X^{2+}}^3}{a_{H^+}^4}$ (activities of $XO_2(s)$ and $H_2O$ are unity)\n\nWith $a_{XO_4^-} = a_{X^{2+}} = 1$:\n\n$Q = \\frac{1}{a_{H^+}^4} = [H^+]^{-4} = 10^{4\\text{pH}}$\n\nWait: $Q = \\frac{1^2 \\cdot 1^3}{a_{H^+}^4} = \\frac{1}{a_{H^+}^4}$\n\nIf we assume $a_{H^+} = 10^{-\\text{pH}}$ (or more precisely, we use the given formula with $[H^+]$):\n\n$\\log Q = \\log \\frac{1}{a_{H^+}^4} = -4\\log a_{H^+} = -4(-\\text{pH}) = 4\\text{pH}$\n\nWait, $\\log a_{H^+} = -\\text{pH}$, so $-4\\log a_{H^+} = 4\\text{pH}$.\n\nSo $Q = 10^{4\\text{pH}}$ and $\\log Q = 4\\text{pH}$.\n\n$E_{\\text{cell}} = 0.300 - \\frac{0.05916}{6} \\times 4\\text{pH} = 0.300 - \\frac{0.23664}{6}\\text{pH} = 0.300 - 0.03944\\text{pH}$\n\nLet me double-check: $\\frac{0.05916 \\times 4}{6} = \\frac{0.23664}{6} = 0.03944$\n\nSo $E_{\\text{cell}} = 0.300 - 0.03944 \\times \\text{pH}$\n\nSetting $E_{\\text{cell}} = 0$:\n$0.300 = 0.03944 \\times \\text{pH}$\n$\\text{pH} = \\frac{0.300}{0.03944} = 7.607...$\n\nLet me compute: $0.300/0.03944 = 7.606...$\n\nMore precisely: $0.3/0.03944 = 7.6065...$\n\nSo at pH \u2248 7.61, $E_{\\text{cell}} = 0$.\n\nFor disproportionation to be thermodynamically favored, we need $E_{\\text{cell}} > 0$, which means:\n$0.300 - 0.03944 \\times \\text{pH} > 0$\n$\\text{pH} < 7.61$\n\nSo disproportionation is favored below pH \u2248 7.61.\n\nWait, let me reconsider. The reaction is:\n$5XO_2(s) + 4H^+ \\rightarrow 2XO_4^- + 3X^{2+} + 2H_2O$\n\n$Q = \\frac{a_{XO_4^-}^2 \\cdot a_{X^{2+}}^3 \\cdot a_{H_2O}^2}{a_{XO_2}^5 \\cdot a_{H^+}^4}$\n\nSince $a_{XO_2} = 1$ (solid) and $a_{H_2O} = 1$ (pure liquid):\n\n$Q = \\frac{a_{XO_4^-}^2 \\cdot a_{X^{2+}}^3}{a_{H^+}^4}$\n\nWith $a_{XO_4^-} = a_{X^{2+}} = 1$:\n\n$Q = \\frac{1}{a_{H^+}^4}$\n\n$\\log Q = -4\\log a_{H^+}$\n\nNow, $a_{H^+} = 10^{-\\text{pH}}$ (in dilute solution, activity \u2248 concentration, and pH = -log $a_{H^+}$)\n\nSo $\\log a_{H^+} = -\\text{pH}$\n\n$\\log Q = -4(-\\text{text{pH}}) = 4\\text{pH}$\n\n$E_{\\text{cell}} = 0.300 - \\frac{0.05916}{6}(4\\text{pH}) = 0.300 - 0.03944\\text{pH}$\n\nThis is correct.\n\nAt pH = 7.61 (approximately), $E_{\\text{cell}} = 0$.\n\nFor pH < 7.61, $E_{\\text{cell}} > 0$, so the forward reaction (disproportionation) is spontaneous.\nFor pH > 7.61, $E_{\\text{cell}} < 0$, so the reverse reaction (comproportionation) is spontaneous.\n\n**(5) At pH = 6.00, $a_{XO_4^-} = 10^{-3}$, $a_{X^{2+}} = 10^{-2}$**\n\n$Q = \\frac{(10^{-3})^2 \\cdot (10^{-2})^3}{(10^{-6})^4} = \\frac{10^{-6} \\cdot 10^{-6}}{10^{-24}} = \\frac{10^{-12}}{10^{-24}} = 10^{12}$\n\n$\\log Q = 12$\n\n$E_{\\text{cell}} = 0.300 - \\frac{0.05916}{6} \\times 12 = 0.300 - 0.05916 \\times 2 = 0.300 - 0.11832 = 0.18168$ V\n\nSo $E_{\\text{cell}} \\approx 0.182$ V > 0, so the disproportionation is spontaneous in the forward direction.\n\n**(6) What's wrong with the student's argument?**\n\nThe student says \"because $E_2^\\circ > E_1^\\circ$, $XO_2$ must disproportionate at every pH and at every composition.\"\n\nThe issue is that $E_2^\\circ > E_1^\\circ$ only tells us that under standard conditions (all activities = 1, i.e., pH = 0), the disproportionation is favorable. But the Nernst equation shows that the actual cell potential depends on pH (through $a_{H^+}$) and on the activities of the products.\n\nSpecifically:\n- The disproportionation reaction consumes $H^+$, so increasing pH (decreasing $a_{H^+}$) makes $Q$ larger, which decreases $E_{\\text{cell}}$.\n- At sufficiently high pH, $E_{\\text{cell}}$ becomes negative, and disproportionation is no longer favorable.\n- Similarly, if the products are present at high activities, $Q$ increases and $E_{\\text{cell}}$ decreases.\n\nSo the student's error is assuming that the comparison of standard potentials (which apply only at standard conditions, i.e., $a_{H^+} = 1$, pH = 0) determines the spontaneity under all conditions. The actual spontaneity depends on the reaction quotient, which depends on pH and the activities of all species.\n\nLet me refine my answer for part (6). The student's statement is wrong because:\n1. $E_2^\\circ > E_1^\\circ$ only establishes that $E_{\\text{cell}}^\\circ > 0$ for the disproportionation, which means the reaction is spontaneous under standard conditions (all solute activities = 1, which implies pH = 0 for the $H^+$).\n2. The Nernst equation shows that the actual $E_{\\text{cell}}$ depends on the reaction quotient $Q$, which depends on pH and the activities of $XO_4^-$ and $X^{2+}$.\n3. At sufficiently high pH (or sufficiently high product activities), $Q$ becomes large enough that $E_{\\text{cell}} < 0$, and the disproportionation is no longer spontaneous.\n\nLet me also double-check part (3) more carefully.\n\n$\\log_{10} K = \\frac{nE_{\\text{cell}}^\\circ}{0.05916} = \\frac{6 \\times 0.300}{0.05916}$\n\n$= \\frac{1.800}{0.05916}$\n\nLet me compute this: $1.800 / 0.05916 = 30.426...$\n\nMore precisely: $0.05916 \\times 30 = 1.7748$, $1.800 - 1.7748 = 0.0252$, $0.0252/0.05916 = 0.4259...$\n\nSo $\\log K = 30.426$ (approximately)\n\n$K = 10^{30.426} = 10^{30} \\times 10^{0.426}$\n\n$10^{0.426}$: We know $10^{0.4} = 2.512$, $10^{0.43} \\approx 2.692$\n\nLet me be more precise: $\\ln(10) \\times 0.426 = 2.3026 \\times 0.426 = 0.9809$\n$e^{0.9809} = 2.667$\n\nSo $K \\approx 2.67 \\times 10^{30}$\n\nActually, I should double-check the formula. The problem states:\n$\\log_{10} K = \\frac{nE_{\\text{cell}}^\\circ}{0.05916}$\n\nThis is the standard relationship at 298.15 K. Let me verify:\n$\\Delta G^\\circ = -nFE_{\\text{cell}}^\\circ$\n$\\Delta G^\\circ = -RT\\ln K$\n$-nFE_{\\text{cell}}^\\circ = -RT\\ln K$\n$\\ln K = \\frac{nFE_{\\text{cell}}^\\circ}{RT}$\n$\\log_{10} K = \\frac{nFE_{\\text{cell}}^\\circ}{2.3026 RT} = \\frac{nE_{\\text{cell}}^\\circ}{2.3026 RT/F}$\n\nAt 298.15 K: $\\frac{2.3026 \\times 8.314 \\times 298.15}{96485} = \\frac{5707.6}{96485} = 0.05916$ V\n\nYes, so $\\log_{10} K = \\frac{nE_{\\text{cell}}^\\circ}{0.05916}$ is correct.\n\nSo $\\log_{10} K = \\frac{6 \\times 0.300}{0.05916} = \\frac{1.800}{0.05916} \\approx 30.43$\n\n$K = 10^{30.43} \\approx 2.7 \\times 10^{30}$\n\nLet me be a bit more precise. $1.8/0.05916 = 30.4259...$\n\n$10^{0.4259} = ?$\n\n$\\log_{10}(2.66) = \\log_{10}(2) + \\log_{10}(1.33) = 0.3010 + 0.1239 = 0.4249$\n$\\log_{10}(2.67) = 0.3010 + \\log_{10}(1.335) = 0.3010 + 0.1255 = 0.4265$\n\nSo $10^{0.4259} \\approx 2.67$\n\n$K \\approx 2.67 \\times 10^{30}$\n\nLet me present the answer more cleanly. I'll use $\\log_{10} K = 30.43$ (to 4 significant figures, since the inputs have 3-4 sig figs).\n\nActually, $0.300$ has 3 sig figs (or could be argued as 3), and $0.05916$ has 4 sig figs. So the result should have about 3 sig figs.\n\n$\\log_{10} K = 30.4$ (3 sig figs)\n\n$K = 10^{30.4} = 2.5 \\times 10^{30}$\n\nHmm, but I think it's better to report $\\log_{10} K$ with a few decimal places and then give $K$ to the appropriate precision. Let me just report $\\log_{10} K = 30.43$ and $K = 2.7 \\times 10^{30}$.\n\nLet me reconsider the significant figures. $E_1^\\circ = 0.900$ V (3 sig figs after decimal), $E_2^\\circ = 1.200$ V (3 sig figs after decimal). $E_{\\text{cell}}^\\circ = 1.200 - 0.900 = 0.300$ V (3 sig figs). \n\n$